         'Munshi Sabir Husain\'s income was small and his expenses large. He could not really afford to keep a nurse for his child, but his worry for the child\'s health on the one hand, and on the other the humiliation of looking inferior to his equals, forced him to bear this expense. The child loved the nurse dearly and was always hanging round her neck, which made her seem even more necessary. But perhaps the biggest reason was that, out of kindness, he did not have the courage to dismiss her. The old woman had been in their service for three years. She had brought up their only child and did her work heart and soul. There was no excuse for sending her away, and slinging mud for no reason was impossible for a gentle man like Sabir. But Shakira did not agree with her husband in this matter. She suspected that the nurse was robbing them. When the nurse came back from the market, Shakira would hide in the doorway to see whether she was hiding away some flour or some firewood. She would examine whatever the nurse brought for hours, regret the purchase, and ask again and again: "Why only this much? What was the price? Has it become so expensive?" Sometimes the nurse answered these suspicions gently, but when the mistress grew too sharp, she too became stiff. She swore oaths and offered proof of her honesty, and hours went in denials and arguments. This went on almost every day, and every day the drama ended with the nurse shedding a few tears. That the nurse stayed on despite all this harshness only watered Shakira\'s suspicions. She could never believe that the old woman stayed merely out of love for the child; she did not think the nurse capable of such a fine feeling.',
